//@ run-pass
//@ requires: madaros

// D17 regression test -- the EC half of the forged-root-DN-collision
// chain-validation bypass.
//
// tests/run-pass/x509_chain_forged_root_dn_collision.sio pins the RSA case,
// closed on 2026-08-25 by making `chain_is_trusted_root` require a
// byte-identical public key on the same trust-store entry, not merely a
// matching Subject DN. That fix compared `modulus` and `public_exponent`
// only -- and `x509_parse_certificate` never populates either for an EC
// certificate (a P-256 key is decoded into `ec_public_key`; both BigInts
// stay at `bigint_zero()`). So for EC the key check was
// `bigint_cmp(zero, zero) == 0` twice, vacuously true, and the bypass was
// still fully open. Measured 2026-08-27: this exact fixture set returned
// CHAIN_OK before the fix, CHAIN_ERR_BAD_SIGNATURE after it.
//
// Fixtures (all P-256, `-days 8400` so the test does not rot; generated
// 2026-08-27):
//   genuine root  CN=d17-ec-root-collision-test, key A -- the ONLY entry
//                 in the trust store below
//   forged root   CN=d17-ec-root-collision-test, key B (the attacker's
//                 own), self-signed, CA:TRUE -- byte-different key,
//                 IDENTICAL Subject DN, which is public information
//   forged leaf   CN=victim.example, issued BY the forged root
//
// The attacker supplies the forged root as an intermediate, exactly as a
// hostile server would in its TLS Certificate message. Accepting it as a
// trust anchor is a complete chain-validation bypass: every signature in
// the path is then the attacker's own, and verifies.
//
// This asserts only that the chain is REJECTED, not the specific error
// code. Post-fix the DFS goes on to try the genuine root as the forged
// root's issuer, so the path is rejected at the signature check rather
// than for having no path at all; pinning that particular code would make
// the test brittle to a legitimate future change in candidate ordering.
// "Not CHAIN_OK" is the security property.

use bignum::bigint::*
use net::socket::*
use x509::cert::*
use x509::chain::*
use x509::trust_store::*

